name: parsing text
intro: Input is text, and text is a pointer you walk: one pointer, a
intro: loop, one decision per character.
intro:
intro:     const char *p = line;
intro:     while (*p == ' ') p++;                  /* skip blanks */
intro:     const char *start = p;
intro:     while (*p != '\0' && *p != ' ') p++;    /* one token */
intro:     size_t len = (size_t)(p - start);       /* its length */
intro:     printf("[%.*s]\n", (int)len, start);    /* print a slice */
intro:
intro: You rarely need to copy: a token is a start pointer and a length,
intro: and `%.*s` prints exactly that many bytes. To keep one, `memcpy`
intro: into a buffer you sized and add the `\0` yourself.
intro:
intro: Do not use `strtok`. It splits by writing NULs into your string and
intro: remembers its place in a hidden static variable: a second tokenizer
intro: anywhere in the program breaks the first, it is not safe with
intro: threads, and it swallows empty fields, so `a,,b` loses one. The
intro: pointer walk costs three lines and has none of those problems.
intro:
intro: Trimming is the same walk from both ends: skip blanks forward, then
intro: step `end` back while the byte before it is blank. Split
intro: `key = value` at the FIRST `=` with `strchr`, so a value may hold
intro: more. Count `\r` as a blank: files from other systems end lines in
intro: `\r\n`.
intro:
intro: CSV adds quoting. A field in double quotes may hold commas, and a
intro: doubled `""` inside it is one quote. Walk it with a small state
intro: machine; unquoting in place is safe, because the result is never
intro: longer than the input.
intro:
intro: Say WHERE input is wrong. A column is `p - line + 1`, 1-based,
intro: counted in bytes: `line 3 col 7: expected )` can be fixed without a
intro: hunt. Errors go to stderr, and `fflush(stdout)` comes first: stdout
intro: is buffered and stderr is not, so on one terminal an error can
intro: otherwise print BEFORE output the program made earlier.
